<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Where should checkout cache tax quotes?</title>
<style>
{css}
</style>
</head>
<body class="louvre">
<main>
<p class="l-kicker">Decision &middot; Payments, by 30 October</p>
<h1>Where should checkout cache tax quotes?</h1>
<p class="l-lede">In Redis, shared by every checkout process. A cart's quote then survives a deploy and a retry on
another instance, for one more network hop of about 2&nbsp;ms.</p>
<p class="l-meta">PAY-151, after PAY-142's fix &middot; canopy worker, for Sam Okafor, 27 October 2026</p>
<h2>What we need</h2>
<p>PAY-142 cut checkout's p95 to 430&nbsp;ms by caching each cart's tax quote for five minutes.</p>
<p>Each checkout process keeps its own cache today, so a deploy empties it, and a retry that lands on another
instance asks the tax service again.</p>
<dl>
<dt>Must</dt><dd>A cached quote is never older than five minutes, or than the cart's promotion.</dd>
<dt>Should</dt><dd>Keep its quotes through a deploy, and share them between instances.</dd>
<dt>Out of scope</dt><dd>Caching shipping rates, which change by the hour.</dd>
</dl>
<h2>The options</h2>
<div class="l-compare">
<div class="l-option">
<h3>In each process</h3>
<p>What we run today: a map in memory, per instance.</p>
<ul>
<li>No new service to run.</li>
<li>Empty after every deploy, and never shared.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="l-option l-chosen">
<h3>In Redis <span class="l-tag">Recommended</span></h3>
<p>One cache, shared by every checkout instance.</p>
<ul>
<li>Survives deploys, and serves retries.</li>
<li>One more service, and 2&nbsp;ms a lookup.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="l-option">
<h3>No cache</h3>
<p>Ask the tax service every time, as before PAY-142.</p>
<ul>
<li>Nothing to invalidate.</li>
<li>Puts back the latency we just removed.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<h2>Side by side</h2>
<div class="l-scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th class="l-num">In process</th><th class="l-num">Redis</th>
<th class="l-num">None</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hit rate, replaying 22 October</td><td class="l-num">61%</td><td class="l-num l-good">94%</td>
<td class="l-num l-bad">0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Added p95</td><td class="l-num">0&nbsp;ms</td><td class="l-num">2&nbsp;ms</td>
<td class="l-num l-bad">380&nbsp;ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tax calls a week</td><td class="l-num">1.2&nbsp;million</td><td class="l-num l-good">190,000</td>
<td class="l-num l-bad">3.1&nbsp;million</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost to run</td><td class="l-num">None</td><td class="l-num">$40 a month</td><td class="l-num">None</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h2>Why Redis</h2>
<p>Redis serves half as many quotes again from cache as the processes do, because deploys and retries no longer
miss.</p>
<p>The tax service bills by the call, so the saving pays for the Redis node many times over.</p>
<p>One small node is enough: the cache holds about 40,000 quotes at the busiest hour, under 30&nbsp;MB.</p>
<div class="l-callout l-risk">If Redis is down, checkout must ask the tax service, not fail. The fallback is part of
the change, and staging tests it with Redis turned off.</div>
<h2>What changes</h2>
<p><del>Each checkout process keeps its own quotes for five minutes.</del> <ins>Every checkout process shares its
quotes in Redis for five minutes.</ins></p>
<h2>If we agree</h2>
<ol class="l-steps">
<li><b>A Redis node for checkout.</b> Platform provisions it in each region, by 31 October.</li>
<li><b>Quotes in Redis, behind a flag.</b> The checkout service reads Redis first and falls back to the tax
service.</li>
<li><b>The flag on, region by region.</b> We watch the hit rate and p95 for a day in each.</li>
</ol>
<footer>
<p>Sources: a replay of 22 October's checkout traffic against each option in staging, and the tax service's
invoice for September.</p>
</footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
